WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.050 --> 00:00:00.763
Welcome.

2
00:00:00.763 --> 00:00:03.612
This briefing comes from
Book 18 of the Federal

3
00:00:03.612 --> 00:00:06.900
Modernization Workbook,
Application Modernization.

4
00:00:06.900 --> 00:00:10.085
It's written for CIOs
and portfolio owners,

5
00:00:10.085 --> 00:00:14.363
architects, data teams, ISSOs
and authorizing officials.

6
00:00:14.363 --> 00:00:15.776
I'll take four questions.

7
00:00:15.776 --> 00:00:18.356
Why is modernizing thousands of legacy

8
00:00:18.356 --> 00:00:21.562
applications a portfolio
decision, not a rewrite?

9
00:00:21.562 --> 00:00:23.624
What should each application become?

10
00:00:23.624 --> 00:00:25.350
Who governs the architecture?

11
00:00:25.350 --> 00:00:26.925
And how do you run it at scale?

12
00:00:26.925 --> 00:00:30.418
The short version: decide
application by application,

13
00:00:30.418 --> 00:00:34.533
build the changed ones on one
architecture, prove the data moved,

14
00:00:34.533 --> 00:00:39.113
give every AI agent an owner, and let
the review gates write the evidence.

15
00:00:39.113 --> 00:00:41.440
Let's start with where the money goes.

16
00:00:42.190 --> 00:00:46.027
This slide matters because it shows
the size of what we're deciding about.

17
00:00:46.027 --> 00:00:50.701
Year after year, about eighty percent
of federal IT spending goes to keeping

18
00:00:50.701 --> 00:00:55.003
existing systems running, and that
share has hardly moved in six years.

19
00:00:55.003 --> 00:00:58.264
When G A O ranked the most
critical legacy systems,

20
00:00:58.264 --> 00:01:01.128
the striking finding
wasn't the technology.

21
00:01:01.128 --> 00:01:02.153
It was planning.

22
00:01:02.153 --> 00:01:06.773
Most had no complete plan, and the
missing pieces were decisions made one

23
00:01:06.773 --> 00:01:11.240
system at a time: milestones, the work,
and what happens to the old system.

24
00:01:11.240 --> 00:01:14.415
And those flagship systems
are only the top of the list.

25
00:01:14.415 --> 00:01:18.377
Most of the cost sits in thousands
of ordinary applications.

26
00:01:18.377 --> 00:01:20.290
So why not just replace them?

27
00:01:21.040 --> 00:01:23.446
The grand rewrite is the obvious answer,

28
00:01:23.446 --> 00:01:26.290
and the federal record
says it rarely ends well.

29
00:01:26.290 --> 00:01:30.728
A rewrite delivers nothing until
cut-over, so users wait years.

30
00:01:30.728 --> 00:01:36.666
Its real specification is the old system,
buried in code, data and workarounds.

31
00:01:36.666 --> 00:01:40.215
And all the risk lands on one
switch with no easy way back.

32
00:01:40.215 --> 00:01:44.879
Federal policy already points the
other way: build in useful segments,

33
00:01:44.879 --> 00:01:48.015
each one delivering a
working system on its own.

34
00:01:48.015 --> 00:01:50.365
That leads to the key idea of the book.

35
00:01:50.365 --> 00:01:54.506
The unit of decision is the application,
because that's where the owner,

36
00:01:54.506 --> 00:01:58.290
the data, the code and the
authorization boundary actually live.

37
00:01:58.290 --> 00:02:01.230
So how do you decide, thousands of times?

38
00:02:01.980 --> 00:02:03.655
Here's the heart of the method.

39
00:02:03.655 --> 00:02:08.470
A study per application would take
longer than most applications have left,

40
00:02:08.470 --> 00:02:12.368
so triage is a rubric applied to
data the agency already holds.

41
00:02:12.368 --> 00:02:15.608
Every application gets
one of six dispositions,

42
00:02:15.608 --> 00:02:20.630
from retire and retain up through
rehost, replatform, refactor and rebuild,

43
00:02:20.630 --> 00:02:24.356
and it gets the cheapest one
that meets its mission need.

44
00:02:24.356 --> 00:02:29.196
A simple table of rules proposes the
answer, owners confirm or contest it,

45
00:02:29.196 --> 00:02:33.230
and deep study is kept for the
high-stakes and disputed cases.

46
00:02:33.230 --> 00:02:37.738
The big surprise for most leaders is
that retire and retain are decisions,

47
00:02:37.738 --> 00:02:40.842
not projects, and that's
where the early savings are.

48
00:02:40.842 --> 00:02:43.530
Then, what do the changed ones become?

49
00:02:44.280 --> 00:02:46.978
If every refactor picks its own stack,

50
00:02:46.978 --> 00:02:51.243
the agency just trades one legacy
problem for many new ones.

51
00:02:51.243 --> 00:02:56.543
So the applications that change land on
one target, reviewed and authorized once.

52
00:02:56.543 --> 00:03:01.125
The book's choices are mainstream
and long-supported: Java services,

53
00:03:01.125 --> 00:03:05.054
React front ends, published A
P I contracts and containers.

54
00:03:05.054 --> 00:03:07.832
They're design guidance,
not a federal rule,

55
00:03:07.832 --> 00:03:10.530
and an agency can substitute its own list.

56
00:03:10.530 --> 00:03:11.905
Two things to remember.

57
00:03:11.905 --> 00:03:16.055
The stack ages fast, so budget
for upgrades as recurring work.

58
00:03:16.055 --> 00:03:20.607
And a refactor happens piece by
piece: a façade sends each request to

59
00:03:20.607 --> 00:03:24.993
the old system or the new one, until
nothing depends on the old one.

60
00:03:24.993 --> 00:03:27.900
The hardest part, though, is the data.

61
00:03:28.650 --> 00:03:33.511
Data is where modernization loses the
most trust, because a missing row or a

62
00:03:33.511 --> 00:03:38.450
lost time of day may not surface until
someone's record is wrong months later.

63
00:03:38.450 --> 00:03:41.609
Code can move a piece at a
time, but data moves once,

64
00:03:41.609 --> 00:03:45.400
and the move isn't done until
you can prove nothing was lost.

65
00:03:45.400 --> 00:03:48.456
The book's answer is
three independent checks:

66
00:03:48.456 --> 00:03:52.075
counts, a checksum, and totals
the business recognizes.

67
00:03:52.075 --> 00:03:53.508
No single check is enough,

68
00:03:53.508 --> 00:03:56.987
because each one shares an
assumption with what it's checking.

69
00:03:56.987 --> 00:04:02.024
And while old and new run side by side,
each table has exactly one writer.

70
00:04:02.024 --> 00:04:05.075
Writing to both databases
is a plan for drift.

71
00:04:05.075 --> 00:04:08.860
Next, the newest part of
the architecture: AI.

72
00:04:09.610 --> 00:04:13.486
AI belongs in the architecture
the same way any component does:

73
00:04:13.486 --> 00:04:17.585
behind one interface, with its
output checked rather than trusted.

74
00:04:17.585 --> 00:04:22.357
An agent is different, because it
acts, so it needs what any actor needs:

75
00:04:22.357 --> 00:04:27.798
an identity, a person who answers for it,
limited permissions and an audit trail.

76
00:04:27.798 --> 00:04:31.323
The design can't count on the
model refusing bad instructions,

77
00:04:31.323 --> 00:04:34.848
since there's no fool-proof
defense against prompt injection.

78
00:04:34.848 --> 00:04:37.451
So the guardrails sit outside the model,

79
00:04:37.451 --> 00:04:41.236
and the rule is simple: no agent
writes to a system of record.

80
00:04:41.236 --> 00:04:46.110
Agents read and propose, people
approve, and services apply the change.

81
00:04:46.110 --> 00:04:48.720
Now, how does all of this stay compliant?

82
00:04:49.470 --> 00:04:53.108
Compliance usually arrives as
a package assembled at the end.

83
00:04:53.108 --> 00:04:55.921
Modernization is the one
moment that can change,

84
00:04:55.921 --> 00:04:59.508
because you're choosing the
platform and the pipeline anyway.

85
00:04:59.508 --> 00:05:04.226
A control the platform carries once is
inherited by every application on it, and

86
00:05:04.226 --> 00:05:09.158
a pipeline that keeps its results produces
evidence without anyone collecting it.

87
00:05:09.158 --> 00:05:10.321
Two more points.

88
00:05:10.321 --> 00:05:14.395
Legacy is itself a compliance
matter: an application kept on

89
00:05:14.395 --> 00:05:18.632
unsupported software needs a plan,
a risk acceptance and a date.

90
00:05:18.632 --> 00:05:22.678
And privacy and records officials
come in at design review,

91
00:05:22.678 --> 00:05:26.807
when a new data store, a new flow
or an AI feature first appears.

92
00:05:26.807 --> 00:05:28.840
So who makes all these calls?

93
00:05:29.590 --> 00:05:34.153
An architecture only holds across
thousands of applications if every

94
00:05:34.153 --> 00:05:38.252
decision has an owner and every
change passes the same few gates.

95
00:05:38.252 --> 00:05:42.315
In the book's design, the review
board reviews; it doesn't vote.

96
00:05:42.315 --> 00:05:47.514
The chief architect owns standards, the
application owner owns the disposition,

97
00:05:47.514 --> 00:05:50.463
the authorizing official
alone accepts risk,

98
00:05:50.463 --> 00:05:53.102
and the C I O approves
what the agency buys.

99
00:05:53.102 --> 00:05:55.127
Every change passes four gates.

100
00:05:55.127 --> 00:05:58.589
The first two need human
judgment, once per application.

101
00:05:58.589 --> 00:06:02.978
The last two run automatically,
many times a day, in the pipeline.

102
00:06:02.978 --> 00:06:05.395
And every gate leaves evidence behind,

103
00:06:05.395 --> 00:06:10.078
so the authorization package is
assembled from proof that already exists.

104
00:06:10.078 --> 00:06:11.530
Let's see it running.

105
00:06:12.280 --> 00:06:16.267
Arguments about architecture are
cheap; code that runs is not.

106
00:06:16.267 --> 00:06:21.596
So the workbook ships a small reference
implementation: one mission application

107
00:06:21.596 --> 00:06:26.142
in miniature, with a service, a front
end, a migration tool and an AI agent.

108
00:06:26.142 --> 00:06:28.254
Each part shows a pattern from the book.

109
00:06:28.254 --> 00:06:32.542
A test keeps the A P I contract
and the code from drifting apart.

110
00:06:32.542 --> 00:06:35.714
The migration tool proves
every load three ways,

111
00:06:35.714 --> 00:06:39.917
and its tests break a value on
purpose to show the checks catch it.

112
00:06:39.917 --> 00:06:43.343
The agent can only file
proposals a person approves.

113
00:06:43.343 --> 00:06:46.981
It's honest about its limits,
too: it's illustrative,

114
00:06:46.981 --> 00:06:51.418
and it hasn't been run against a
real D B two or Oracle database.

115
00:06:51.418 --> 00:06:54.150
How does this fit an architecture office?

116
00:06:54.900 --> 00:06:59.961
Many agencies have an enterprise architecture
office that speaks TOGAF or Zachman,

117
00:06:59.961 --> 00:07:02.925
and this slide is about
not making them translate.

118
00:07:02.925 --> 00:07:06.790
TOGAF gives the order to do the
work in, and the book's triage,

119
00:07:06.790 --> 00:07:11.138
target architecture and wave plan
drop straight into its phases.

120
00:07:11.138 --> 00:07:15.544
Zachman is a checklist: it shows
whether you've described everything,

121
00:07:15.544 --> 00:07:18.250
and an empty cell is a
question, not a defect.

122
00:07:18.250 --> 00:07:22.579
But the federal requirement behind
both is the architecture itself,

123
00:07:22.579 --> 00:07:25.162
a baseline, a target
and a plan to get there.

124
00:07:25.162 --> 00:07:27.712
No rule requires either framework.

125
00:07:27.712 --> 00:07:30.100
So file the book's artifacts as they are.

126
00:07:30.100 --> 00:07:32.900
That brings us to the plan: waves.

127
00:07:33.650 --> 00:07:37.738
A disposition for every
application is a list, not a plan.

128
00:07:37.738 --> 00:07:40.267
Thousands of projects can't start at once,

129
00:07:40.267 --> 00:07:43.688
and one at a time keeps
rediscovering the same problems.

130
00:07:43.688 --> 00:07:44.963
Waves solve both.

131
00:07:44.963 --> 00:07:49.092
Start with a pilot that includes
one genuinely hard application,

132
00:07:49.092 --> 00:07:51.463
so the first real wave isn't a surprise.

133
00:07:51.463 --> 00:07:54.392
Then run factory waves that
get cheaper each time,

134
00:07:54.392 --> 00:07:56.750
and actually switch the old systems off.

135
00:07:56.750 --> 00:07:58.982
Waves are also how you fund the work:

136
00:07:58.982 --> 00:08:02.138
one wave's retirement
savings can pay for the next.

137
00:08:02.138 --> 00:08:06.371
Size waves by what the factory really
delivers, not by the schedule,

138
00:08:06.371 --> 00:08:09.450
and count savings only once
the old system is off.

139
00:08:09.450 --> 00:08:11.290
So, what should you decide?

140
00:08:12.040 --> 00:08:13.415
So, four decisions.

141
00:08:13.415 --> 00:08:15.945
Ask for the inventory before the program:

142
00:08:15.945 --> 00:08:19.590
an owner and a proposed
disposition for every application.

143
00:08:19.590 --> 00:08:24.702
Approve the rubric once, rather than
each decision, and fund the retirements.

144
00:08:24.702 --> 00:08:30.190
Before any cut-over, ask to see the three
proof results, and forbid dual writes.

145
00:08:30.190 --> 00:08:32.823
And make sure every AI agent has a sponsor

146
00:08:32.823 --> 00:08:35.378
and no power to change
a record on its own.

147
00:08:35.378 --> 00:08:36.990
The next step is small.

148
00:08:36.990 --> 00:08:41.695
Score a pilot set of applications your
architects already know well, calibrate

149
00:08:41.695 --> 00:08:46.328
the rubric against their judgment, and pick
a first wave with one hard case in it.

150
00:08:46.328 --> 00:08:50.064
Book 18 has the detail,
every claim footnoted.

151
00:08:50.064 --> 00:08:50.890
Thank you.
